-Estimates the Jacobian matrix using central differences. Choose this when volume deformation or density change rates are needed, rather than just surface normals.
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+- **Grid Size:** The extrapolation formula requires at least 3 voxels in each dimension to access $V_{n-2}$ and $V_{n-3}$ (or equivalent indices). If the grid is smaller than 3 voxels in any dimension, the method fails.
+- **Boundary Continuity:** The choice of extrapolation affects gradient continuity at edges. The 4th-order extrapolation ensures smoothness but assumes the SDF is well-behaved near the boundary [G].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N_x N_y N_z)$, as it performs a constant number of operations per voxel [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_x N_y N_z)$ for storing the input grid and three output gradient tensors [G].
-- **Strengths:** Maintains 4th-order accuracy at boundaries, reducing artifacts in ray marching compared to 2nd-order schemes [G].
-- **Limitations:** Requires a minimum grid resolution of 3. More complex to implement than 2nd-order stencils due to boundary handling [G].
-- **When to use:** High-quality SDF rendering where boundary artifacts are visible with lower-order methods [G].
-- **When not to use:** Real-time applications with very low resolution grids where the overhead of boundary logic is disproportionate, or when $N < 3$ [G].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+- Time: $O(N_x N_y N_z)$, as each voxel is processed a constant number of times.
+- Memory: $O(N_x N_y N_z)$ for the input and output grids. Temporary arrays for extrapolated boundaries are negligible.
+
+**Measured Performance:**
+- Performance depends on the hardware and implementation. The method is vectorizable and efficient on GPUs.
+- Scaling: Linear with respect to the number of voxels.
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+- Strengths: High accuracy (4th-order) even at boundaries. Smooth normals improve ray marching stability and shading quality.
+- Limitations: Requires a minimum grid size of 3 voxels per dimension. More complex to implement than 2nd-order central differences.
+
+**When to Use:**
+- Use when high-quality normals are required for SDF-based rendering or physics.
+- Avoid when grid resolution is very low (<3 voxels) or when computational resources are extremely constrained and lower accuracy is acceptable.
 
 ## Background
-This method is implemented in the SDFDiff repository, associated with research on differentiable rendering of SDFs [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the need for accurate surface normals in volume rendering pipelines that rely on Signed Distance Functions [G].
